WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.544
(light dissonant music)

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:04.367
- I pretty much had an image in my mind

00:00:04.367 --> 00:00:06.120
that most people in the world are Jewish,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.122
and most Jews are ultra-Orthodox.

00:00:09.122 --> 00:00:13.141
When I was 19 was the first
time I got access to internet,

00:00:13.141 --> 00:00:16.308
and I was like, okay, I have to check.

00:00:18.069 --> 00:00:22.989
I found the right Hebrew
Wikipedia page about trans people.

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.870
I couldn't read English at that point,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.938
I was only able to read in Hebrew.

00:00:26.938 --> 00:00:29.102
My life was never the same after that.

00:00:29.102 --> 00:00:31.964
(light ambient music)

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:34.255
I'm Abby Stein, I was raised
in the Hasidic Community

00:00:34.255 --> 00:00:36.634
in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, New York.

00:00:36.634 --> 00:00:40.134
I identify as a woman of trans experience.

00:00:42.849 --> 00:00:45.181
The Hasidic community in Williamsburg

00:00:45.181 --> 00:00:47.326
is probably one of the
most sheltered communities,

00:00:47.326 --> 00:00:50.900
definitely in the US,
perhaps even in the world.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.928
Everything is different.

00:00:52.928 --> 00:00:55.064
(light dissonant music)

00:00:55.064 --> 00:00:58.649
I was raised without internet, TV, movies,

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:01.399
Broadway shows, music, magazines.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.507
I couldn't have a conversation
in English until I was 20.

00:01:08.163 --> 00:01:11.566
In the Hasidic community,
men shave their heads.

00:01:11.566 --> 00:01:13.346
I hated that I had to shave,

00:01:13.346 --> 00:01:15.474
I would have loved to have long hair.

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:17.234
Now it's just as different as it gets,

00:01:17.234 --> 00:01:21.401
at least in terms of facial
hair or, like, anything like that.

00:01:23.854 --> 00:01:26.771
Growing up, I always identified more

00:01:27.609 --> 00:01:30.933
with the feminine part, with the females

00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:33.433
in my family and my community.

00:01:34.271 --> 00:01:36.593
Every time that I tried to tell myself,

00:01:36.593 --> 00:01:39.538
it's not that you're a girl,
something else is the problem,

00:01:39.538 --> 00:01:42.097
I would start battling with depression.

00:01:42.097 --> 00:01:44.014
And it got to a point where I realized,

00:01:44.014 --> 00:01:47.864
hey, this is not a life,
I have to do something.

00:01:47.864 --> 00:01:50.655
When I left the community that I was raised in,

00:01:50.655 --> 00:01:53.259
I didn't know how to dress
or how to talk to people,

00:01:53.259 --> 00:01:56.045
and I had to figure out
everything from scratch.

00:01:56.045 --> 00:01:58.928
And in some ways that's
exactly what I'm doing now

00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:02.728
in terms of transition
on the gender spectrum.

00:02:02.728 --> 00:02:05.251
People tell me a lot, oh, you're so brave,

00:02:05.251 --> 00:02:07.756
you're so strong, and I always feel like,

00:02:07.756 --> 00:02:12.243
I don't know, I only did
what I have to do to survive.

00:02:12.243 --> 00:02:14.707
And if there's one thing
that I could go back

00:02:14.707 --> 00:02:17.283
and tell myself when I was six years old,

00:02:17.283 --> 00:02:21.661
12 years old, it's know there
are other people like you.

00:02:21.661 --> 00:02:24.911
(light ambient music)

